## Protocol Boundary

The protocol rotates authority without mutating either membership or root in
place. It retains the old and new Witness Mesh v1 proof objects and joins their
compact native summaries. The private linear Sounio receipt carries both epoch
numbers, membership and root digests, the shared domain/checkpoint, both anchor
sequences, both certificate digests, and the predecessor digest. The separate
persisted OCaml receipt additionally contains the canonical old and new
certificate bytes, signed current status receipts, membership-file and root
identities, shared checkpoint, and predecessor path. Verification reloads the
retained files and certificate chains; neither receipt is accepted as an
assertion about missing state.

Epoch one is the explicit bootstrap boundary: it may prepare `1 -> 2` without a
prior active pointer, and records the all-zero predecessor digest. Every later
handoff must resolve the active pointer, recursively verify the preceding
handoff chain, and show that its new membership/root are the next handoff's old
membership/root. This v0 admits `from_epoch` values 1 through 64, hence at most
64 transitions and active epochs through 65. Longer histories require a future
compaction or roll-forward authority. The verifier refuses an absent,
malformed, substituted, inconsistent, or out-of-range predecessor chain.

## Crash Semantics

Preparation writes a deterministic per-epoch receipt with file fsync, rename,
and directory fsync. Activation writes a separate canonical pointer with the
same sequence. A crash after preparation leaves no active epoch and retry
reuses the already verified receipt. A crash after activation leaves the new
pointer visible and retry returns the same transition as idempotent. A conflicting
prepared receipt or active transition refuses; it is never overwritten.

This proves process-crash recovery, not adversarial freshness of a filesystem
snapshot. If an attacker can roll the epoch-control directory back together
with every referenced receipt, a self-consistent older pointer may replay. A
meaningful rollback-resistance deployment must put that directory under a
monotonic authority outside the attacker's rollback domain.

## Native And Linear Boundary

Frame `9015` has seven independently sabotaged rules:

- `witness_epoch_quorums_are_verified` requires both verified flags and exact
  `3/4 + 3/4` configuration summaries;
- `witness_epoch_epochs_are_adjacent` requires `1 <= from_epoch <= 64` and
  `to_epoch = from_epoch + 1`;
- `witness_epoch_memberships_change` rejects a reused membership digest;
- `witness_epoch_roots_are_separated` rejects a reused root identity;
- `witness_epoch_checkpoint_agrees` requires both anchor sequences to be
  positive (they may differ) and binds domain, event count, and journal head
  across both sides;
- `witness_epoch_certificates_are_bound` requires nonzero distinct boundary
  certificate digests;
- `witness_epoch_chain_is_well_formed` checks the native digest shape: it
  permits a zero predecessor only from epoch one and requires a nonzero
  predecessor thereafter. The OCaml verifier must resolve that digest and bind
  the full predecessor chain, so frame 9015 is necessary but not sufficient.

One epoch-bound `VerifiedOldEpochQuorum` and one epoch-bound
`VerifiedNewEpochQuorum`, each admitted only from an exact `verified=1`,
`required=3`, `members=4` summary under the host-trust effect, are consumed to construct one
`VerifiedWitnessEpochHandoff`. Rebinding those tokens to caller-supplied epoch
numbers refuses. The proof is consumed once to obtain the private host receipt.
External host-receipt construction refuses with `E176`; reusing either quorum
proof refuses with `E039`.

## Evidence Boundary

The PL/CS contribution implemented here is a small proof-carrying
reconfiguration kernel: linear old/new authority tokens, an independently
sabotage-tested refinement frame, retained certificate-chain replay, and a
crash-recoverable activation commit. It is an executable research mechanism,
not a literature-priority claim. Any novelty comparison with Vertical Paxos,
joint consensus, reconfiguration logics, or proof-carrying distributed systems
requires a separate sourced related-work review.

## Falsification Conditions

- one current quorum can authorize rotation without the other;
- a skipped, repeated, or descending epoch activates;
- a 65th transition activates under this bounded v0;
- an epoch-bound quorum token can be rebound to different caller inputs;
- old and new membership or root identity can be reused;
- different event counts or journal heads activate;
- a boundary certificate, current status receipt, retained membership file, or
  retained root can change without invalidating the handoff;
- a non-genesis handoff verifies without its predecessor;
- a crash after preparation loses the receipt or publishes the new active epoch;
- a crash after activation makes exact retry create a second transition;
- a conflicting prepared receipt is overwritten;
- removing one named frame-9015 rule fails to admit its exact sabotage control;
- a report calls the result dynamic consensus, asynchronous liveness, automatic
  state transfer, physical truth, or rollback-safe while the control directory
  remains under the attacked rollback authority.
